digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "N1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently human respiratory viruses such as influenza virus (IV) and respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) are important causes of severe respiratory infections."];
  "N10" [label="(7,0,0) Currently similar asynchronous resurgence was also noted in other countries with varied timing and stridency of NPIs."];
  "N12" [label="(0,0,0) The background knowledge is that variations in viral resurgence could be driven by regionally specific factors (like local NPIs) or globally consistent viral characteristics, and understanding this is crucial for future pandemic preparedness."];
  "N14" [label="(0,0,0) If it remains unclear whether the observed variations in the resurgence of these respiratory viruses are regionally specific or globally consistent, then we should compile a large dataset on respiratory virus activity across the globe."];
  "N16" [label="(12,0,0) If we compiled a large dataset on respiratory virus activity across the globe from published studies, surveillance reports, and international collaborators, then data were collected from three sources: systematic literature review, online surveillance reports, and the RSV GEN network."];
  "N18" [label="(10,0,0) If we successfully gathered global data from literature, surveillance, and the RSV GEN network, then we determined the onset and peak of epidemics from different data sources using a unified method."];
  "N2" [label="(2,0,0) If human respiratory viruses such as influenza virus (IV) and respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) are important causes of severe respiratory infections, then previous reports showed that most of the common respiratory viruses had distinct seasonal circulating patterns albeit with variations globally by geographical and environmental characteristics."];
  "N20" [label="(13,0,0) If we determined the onset and peak of epidemics using a unified method, then the epidemic onset was defined using a '4-week trend' method, where each week’s viral activity was compared to that of four weeks prior."];
  "N22" [label="(14,0,0) If we applied the '4-week trend' method to define epidemic onsets, then an epidemic onset was identified when four consecutive weeks showed higher activity than their respective weeks four weeks earlier."];
  "N24" [label="(11,0,0) If epidemic onsets were identified based on four consecutive weeks of higher activity, then we used a generalised linear mixed-effects model (GLMM) framework to synthesise the time interval between epidemic onsets and between epidemic peaks of different respiratory viruses during the COVID-19 pandemic."];
  "N4" [label="(3,0,0) If common respiratory viruses have distinct and monitored seasonal circulating patterns globally, then the onset of the COVID-19 pandemic and the large-scale implementation of NPIs across the globe was associated with a precipitous and major impact on the circulation of common respiratory viruses, keeping their activity remarkably lower."];
  "N6" [label="(4,0,0) If the normal seasonal circulating patterns of respiratory viruses were severely disrupted by COVID-19 NPIs, then the subsequent relaxation of NPIs resulted in an unprecedented out-of-season resurgence of RSV in several countries, likely a result of depleted population immunity."];
  "N8" [label="(6,0,0) If the relaxation of NPIs led to an out-of-season resurgence of respiratory viruses, then for example, according to the weekly sentinel surveillance report from New South Wales, Australia, respiratory viruses resurged at different times, with RV/AdV least affected, RSV resurging in Sept 2020, PIV/MPV in mid-2021, and IV remaining low."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This study investigates whether out-of-season post-COVID-19 resurgences of common respiratory viruses after relaxation of non-pharmaceutical interventions represent region-specific anomalies or a consistent global pattern by compiling worldwide surveillance and literature data, uniformly defining epidemic onsets and epidemic peaks, and using generalized linear mixed-effects models to compare onset-to-peak timing intervals across viruses and locations.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Evidence of disrupted and asynchronous respiratory-virus resurgence motivates a global, uniformly analyzed comparison of epidemic timing intervals to distinguish local anomalies from broader post-pandemic patterns."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Most common human respiratory viruses, including influenza virus and RSV, have distinct seasonal circulating patterns reported in prior work."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) The study applied a 4-week trend method to define epidemic onsets, comparing each week’s viral activity with activity four weeks earlier."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Epidemic onsets were identified when four consecutive weeks showed higher activity than their respective weeks four weeks earlier."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) The study used a generalized linear mixed-effects model framework to synthesize timing intervals between epidemic onsets and epidemic peaks."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) The onset of the COVID-19 pandemic and large-scale implementation of non-pharmaceutical interventions were associated with major disruption of the usual seasonal circulation of common respiratory viruses."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) After COVID-19 non-pharmaceutical interventions were relaxed, RSV, a respiratory virus, showed an unprecedented out-of-season resurgence in several settings."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) In New South Wales, Australia, sentinel surveillance reported that respiratory viruses resurged at different times in the post-NPI-relaxation resurgence context."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Across New South Wales and other countries with varied timing and stringency of NPIs, common respiratory viruses showed asynchronous resurgence rather than simultaneous resurgence."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Because common respiratory-virus resurgences were asynchronous, it remained unclear whether the observed variation reflected region-specific factors such as local NPIs or globally consistent viral characteristics."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) To investigate whether post-pandemic respiratory-virus resurgence patterns were region-specific or globally consistent, the study compiled a large dataset on respiratory-virus activity across the globe."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) The study gathered worldwide respiratory-virus activity data from published literature, surveillance reports, and the RSV GEN network."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Using the gathered worldwide data, the study determined epidemic onsets and epidemic peaks from different data sources with a unified method."];
  "N2" -> "R1" [label="deduction-rule"];
